1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,931
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,931 --> 00:00:06,979
This is chapter sixteen: generics in depth.

3
00:00:07,429 --> 00:00:13,428
Most developers use generics fluently for years without learning the model underneath.

4
00:00:13,428 --> 00:00:15,869
That's fine until you write an API.

5
00:00:15,869 --> 00:00:18,520
Then the questions arrive all at once.

6
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:21,729
Why isn't a list of strings a list of objects?

7
00:00:21,729 --> 00:00:23,612
Why is new T array illegal?

8
00:00:23,612 --> 00:00:25,007
And what is capture?

9
00:00:25,907 --> 00:00:29,506
Before generics, every collection was a bag of Object.

10
00:00:29,506 --> 00:00:40,435
You cast on every read, and when someone added the wrong type, the ClassCastException surfaced at the get, possibly in a different class, far from the real mistake.

11
00:00:40,885 --> 00:00:46,929
Generics moved the check to compile time, right at the mistake, and removed the casts.

12
00:00:46,929 --> 00:00:52,060
But Java 5 had to stay binary compatible with existing libraries and VMs.

13
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:58,315
That constraint produced erasure, and erasure produced every restriction in this chapter.

14
00:00:59,215 --> 00:01:00,182
Here's erasure.

15
00:01:00,182 --> 00:01:01,860
You write a List of Order.

16
00:01:02,310 --> 00:01:04,571
The compiler checks every use of it.

17
00:01:05,021 --> 00:01:07,802
Then it throws the type arguments away.

18
00:01:07,802 --> 00:01:12,723
List of Order becomes plain List, and T becomes Object, or its bound.

19
00:01:13,173 --> 00:01:16,239
And it inserts casts wherever you read a value.

20
00:01:16,239 --> 00:01:21,002
So at runtime, a list of strings and a list of orders are the same class.

21
00:01:21,452 --> 00:01:23,309
Every restriction follows.

22
00:01:23,309 --> 00:01:28,738
You can't write new T, or a new T array, because there's no type at runtime.

23
00:01:28,738 --> 00:01:31,809
You can't test instanceof a List of String.

24
00:01:31,809 --> 00:01:37,952
A static field can't be of type T, because one class is shared by every instantiation.

25
00:01:37,952 --> 00:01:42,167
And two overloads that differ only in type arguments clash.

26
00:01:43,067 --> 00:01:44,605
Generics are invariant.

27
00:01:44,605 --> 00:01:49,419
A List of Order is not a List of Object, even though Order is an Object.

28
00:01:49,419 --> 00:01:50,155
Here's why.

29
00:01:50,155 --> 00:01:56,976
If you could assign it, you could add a string through the object view, and poison the list of orders.

30
00:01:57,426 --> 00:02:00,272
So invariance is what makes generics safe.

31
00:02:00,272 --> 00:02:05,490
And wildcards are how you get flexibility back without giving up that safety.

32
00:02:06,390 --> 00:02:11,172
The rule for wildcards is PECS: producer extends, consumer super.

33
00:02:11,172 --> 00:02:16,763
A parameter you read from is a producer, so give it question mark extends T.

34
00:02:16,763 --> 00:02:22,134
A parameter you write to is a consumer, so give it question mark super T.

35
00:02:22,584 --> 00:02:24,958
The payoff is at the call site.

36
00:02:24,958 --> 00:02:30,089
Sum reads cart lines, so it accepts a list of any CartLine subtype.

37
00:02:30,089 --> 00:02:35,832
collectSkus writes SKUs, so it accepts a list of Object as its destination.

38
00:02:35,832 --> 00:02:38,819
Without the wildcards, both calls fail.

39
00:02:39,269 --> 00:02:42,545
If you both read and write, use T exactly.

40
00:02:42,545 --> 00:02:45,042
The JDK follows this everywhere.

41
00:02:45,042 --> 00:02:49,957
Collections dot copy uses both wildcards in a single signature.

42
00:02:50,857 --> 00:02:53,702
PECS applies to functional parameters too.

43
00:02:53,702 --> 00:03:00,478
Here's a generic Result type: a sealed interface with success and failure records, and a map method.

44
00:03:00,928 --> 00:03:07,320
Look at map's parameter: a Function of question mark super T, question mark extends R.

45
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:09,996
The function consumes a T, so super.

46
00:03:09,996 --> 00:03:12,152
It produces an R, so extends.

47
00:03:12,152 --> 00:03:18,916
That way a function from Object to String is a perfectly good mapper for a Result of Order.

48
00:03:18,916 --> 00:03:22,112
Every map in the JDK is declared like this.

49
00:03:23,012 --> 00:03:25,010
Arrays and generics disagree.

50
00:03:25,010 --> 00:03:33,829
Arrays are covariant, so an Order array can be viewed as an Object array, and storing a string compiles, then throws at runtime.

51
00:03:33,829 --> 00:03:36,791
Generics chose compile-time safety instead.

52
00:03:36,791 --> 00:03:38,583
So new T array is illegal.

53
00:03:38,583 --> 00:03:47,402
The workaround is casting an Object array, which is what ArrayList does internally, but only in a private field, never returned.

54
00:03:47,402 --> 00:03:49,124
Usually, just use a List.

55
00:03:49,574 --> 00:03:56,200
Varargs of T create exactly such an array, which is why the compiler warns about heap pollution.

56
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:59,376
SafeVarargs suppresses it, but it's a promise.

57
00:03:59,376 --> 00:04:05,105
If the array escapes, like returning it here, the caller gets a ClassCastException.

58
00:04:06,005 --> 00:04:10,743
Because of erasure, a method can't know its own T unless you pass it.

59
00:04:10,743 --> 00:04:14,794
A Class of T works for simple types, like reading a Config.

60
00:04:15,244 --> 00:04:18,314
But List dot class loses its type argument.

61
00:04:18,314 --> 00:04:20,884
So libraries use a super type token.

62
00:04:20,884 --> 00:04:25,524
Generic information about a superclass is kept in the class file.

63
00:04:25,524 --> 00:04:31,949
Create an anonymous subclass, note the braces, and reflection can read List of Order back.

64
00:04:31,949 --> 00:04:35,447
That's exactly how Jackson's TypeReference works.

65
00:04:36,347 --> 00:04:37,510
Three internals.

66
00:04:37,510 --> 00:04:44,051
Erasure removes type arguments from bytecode, but declarations keep a Signature attribute.

67
00:04:44,051 --> 00:04:48,412
So reflection can see that a method returns a List of Order.

68
00:04:48,412 --> 00:04:52,773
An instance's type arguments, though, genuinely don't exist.

69
00:04:53,223 --> 00:04:54,279
Bridge methods.

70
00:04:54,279 --> 00:04:58,148
A Comparator of Order erases to compare of two Objects.

71
00:04:58,148 --> 00:05:03,284
So the compiler generates a bridge that casts and calls your real method.

72
00:05:03,284 --> 00:05:10,461
That's how polymorphism survives erasure, and why stack traces sometimes show methods you never wrote.

73
00:05:10,911 --> 00:05:11,729
And capture.

74
00:05:11,729 --> 00:05:20,588
The compiler names each wildcard occurrence separately, so it can't prove that what you read and what you write are the same type.

75
00:05:20,588 --> 00:05:26,313
The fix is always to delegate to a generic helper method, which names the type once.

76
00:05:27,213 --> 00:05:28,420
Production rules.

77
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:30,549
Apply PECS to every parameter.

78
00:05:30,549 --> 00:05:38,215
Use wildcards in parameters, but concrete types in return values, so callers don't inherit capture problems.

79
00:05:38,215 --> 00:05:41,267
Only bound T when the bound does real work.

80
00:05:41,267 --> 00:05:43,609
Prefer lists over generic arrays.

81
00:05:43,609 --> 00:05:46,165
Comment every unchecked suppression.

82
00:05:46,165 --> 00:05:49,997
And turn on the raw types and unchecked lint warnings.

83
00:05:50,447 --> 00:05:51,873
Never use raw types.

84
00:05:51,873 --> 00:05:56,007
They switch off generic checking for the whole expression.

85
00:05:56,007 --> 00:05:59,570
Never return a T array built from an Object array.

86
00:05:59,570 --> 00:06:03,704
Never overload on signatures that erase to the same thing.

87
00:06:03,704 --> 00:06:08,907
Don't put wildcards in field types, or mix arrays and generics in an API.

88
00:06:08,907 --> 00:06:12,399
And never use SafeVarargs when the array escapes.

89
00:06:13,299 --> 00:06:19,486
Java 5 introduced the whole model: generics, erasure, wildcards and bridge methods.

90
00:06:19,936 --> 00:06:26,080
Java 7 added the diamond operator, and Java 9 allowed it on anonymous classes.

91
00:06:26,530 --> 00:06:32,482
Java 10's var works with generics, but never with a bare diamond, which infers Object.

92
00:06:32,932 --> 00:06:39,396
Records, sealed types and record patterns all work with generics, as the Result example showed.

93
00:06:39,846 --> 00:06:47,987
And Project Valhalla is working on value types and specialised generics, which would allow a List of int without boxing.

94
00:06:47,987 --> 00:06:51,515
It isn't delivered, and any timeline is speculation.

95
00:06:52,415 --> 00:06:53,051
To recap.

96
00:06:53,051 --> 00:07:01,100
Erasure means types are checked at compile time, then discarded, and that one decision explains every restriction.

97
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:05,830
Invariance keeps generics safe, and wildcards add flexibility back.

98
00:07:05,830 --> 00:07:11,408
Remember PECS: read with extends, write with super, and use T when you do both.

99
00:07:11,408 --> 00:07:15,221
And arrays are covariant and unsound, so prefer lists.

100
00:07:15,671 --> 00:07:17,848
That completes the Language section.

101
00:07:17,848 --> 00:07:23,656
Next is the Collections section, starting with chapter seventeen: choosing the right collection.

102
00:07:23,656 --> 00:07:24,865
Thanks for watching.
